INT. NOTARY'S OFFICE - DAY

A cramped room above a dry cleaner. Steam pipes bang. A wall calendar shows a lighthouse. One desk, three chairs, a stack of papers between them.

GLORIA TANAKA-REYES, 50s, in a navy suit, sits rigid, hands flat on the desk.

LEONARD FITCH, 60s, the notary, adjusts his bow tie and squints at the will.

EZEKIEL "ZEKE" TANAKA-REYES, 30s, her younger brother, slumps in the third chair, jiggling his knee.

LEONARD
The house goes to the surviving child.

GLORIA
There are two of us.

LEONARD
The surviving child who occupied the house for the last five years.

ZEKE
That's me.

GLORIA
That's you because I was working.

ZEKE
(to Leonard)
I was there when she fell.

GLORIA
(to the room)
We were all there when she fell.

ZEKE
(under his breath)
Not you.

GLORIA
Say that again.

LEONARD
Please. The language of the will is clear.

GLORIA
Then read the clause about the debts.

LEONARD
The debts also go to the occupant.

ZEKE
Wait, what debts?

Leonard slides a page across. Zeke stares at it. His knee stops.

ZEKE (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Oh, Mama. What did you do.
